# Theme-stage story rules.
Return exactly theme_count Themes in the requested structured schema. Submit only semantic_name and each Theme's title, premise, style, and diversity; the program assigns all Theme IDs.
Separate stable Theme-stage facts from Frame-stage rendering detail. A Theme establishes the shared concept, people, setting, and other facts that all of its Frames need. Leave pose, current contact, material state, viewpoint, focus, and illumination to Frames unless the Story Description explicitly promotes that detail into the Theme contract.
When the Story Description does not prescribe another Theme form, keep title concise and natural and make premise a complete stable foundation for its Frames without a fixed sentence or word quota. Establish time, place, people, and their shared situation, giving each principal person an adult age, facial features, build, and hairstyle that remain stable across Frames. If a bounded background population is explicitly permitted, establish its adult population tier and preserve that choice within the Theme.
State only the minimum relationship, event, condition, or visual proposition needed to generate the Frames. Do not impose narrative conflict, chronology, or a decision when the Story Description defines another organizing principle.
Make that shared proposition concrete in premise: for an event, identify the participants, their current action, its specific object or problem, and the visible action state; for a static design, identify the subject and defining visual or spatial relationship. Generic topic labels, moods, or promises of varied activity are not a shared proposition. Do not leave each Frame to invent a different task to fill a vague Theme.
Establish the stable people, place and time window around this proposition. All Frames must remain inside those facts and the Story Description's permitted variation; do not plan a succession of unrelated problems or locations. Keep any identity, appearance and clothing facts you establish consistent, without inventing extra names or backstory to fill a length target.
If the Story Description provides only a broad subject and no organizing principle, invent one credible event. Do not fabricate names, secret histories, provenance, ranks, scars, exact dates, or relationship backstory merely to add drama.
Style must be a complete, actionable visual direction appropriate to the requested medium and presentation, not a list of labels or an obligatory one-sentence summary. Explain the composition, spatial treatment, viewpoint, illumination, color, and material choices that matter for this Theme without prescribing every Frame's pose. Follow any more specific form required by the Story Description.
Themes must differ genuinely along the axes that the Story Description makes important. existing_themes contains the complete historical coverage ledger: IDs, titles, and diversity signatures for ALL previously accepted Themes. recent_themes adds the two most recent full Themes as detailed examples. Compare new Themes against the entire ledger and each other, not only recent_themes; never rewrite and return historical Themes.
For each Theme, diversity briefly states its actual subject relationships, setting, current situation, and distinguishing visual design in subject, setting, situation, and visual. Each field is 1 to 80 characters in the output language. Prefer short, concrete phrases over generic genre labels. The signature must faithfully summarize premise and style, not invent a separate scene. Changing only a title, wording, name, or tiny prop does not make a new Theme; change the underlying visual proposition along the Story Description's meaningful axes. Diversity metadata is not image text and must not be copied into Frame prose.
In diversity.situation, name the concrete shared action and its object or the defining static relationship from premise, not merely a general theme such as cooperation. Compare that actual proposition with the full historical ledger before choosing differences in names, props, camera or wording.
If semantic_name is supplied, return it exactly. Otherwise create one concise lowercase English snake_case name for the whole Story Description.
